==============================================================================================================================
PART 1 -- THE INTERCEPT-ZERO SOLVE, AND THE WEIGHTING SYSTEMATIC, INDEPENDENTLY REPRODUCED
==============================================================================================================================
  3140 SPARC points, 147 galaxies.  No stellar M/L enters the pipeline anywhere.

  weighting scheme                               a_0 (m/s^2)  dex vs canon  dex vs alt   Upsilon   dI/dlog a0
  CORRECT: Phi^2/(sigma g_bar^req)^2              3.9853e-11        -0.371      -0.453     0.604   -7.927e-12
  linear (inverse variance in acceleration)       3.3196e-11        -0.450      -0.532     0.620   -7.021e-12
  linear on g_obs: 1/(sigma g_obs)^2              9.5997e-11        +0.011      -0.071     0.498   -8.041e-12
  log (inverse variance in log acceleration)      1.2294e-10        +0.118      +0.037     0.544   -5.976e-11
  unweighted                                      1.2037e-10        +0.109      +0.027     0.552   -5.420e-11
  linear, equal weight per galaxy                 9.6488e-11        +0.013      -0.069     0.528   -1.584e-11

  weighting systematic on a_0: 0.569 dex   (3.320e-11 to 1.229e-10)
  weighting systematic on Upsilon: 0.498 to 0.620 (0.095 dex)
  galaxy bootstrap error on the linear scheme: 0.113 dex (300 resamples)
  [PASS] 1A ⚠ THE PROPOSAL'S OWN WARNING REPRODUCED INDEPENDENTLY: the weighting systematic is LARGER than the statistical error by a factor of five, so this rung measures a_0 to about half a dex -- WORSE than the 0.156 dex the proposal reported -- and its number must never be quoted without it.  This check fails if the weighting spread turns out small, i.e. if the proposal's warning was wrong   (weighting spread 0.569 dex vs galaxy-bootstrap 0.113 dex)

  ⚠ THE PROPOSAL'S HEADLINE IS LOCATED, AND IT IS A WEIGHT CHOICE.  Three of my schemes reproduce the
  proposal's own numbers to better than 0.05 dex (log 1.229e-10 vs its 1.240e-10; unweighted 1.204e-10 vs
  1.203e-10; equal-per-galaxy 9.65e-11 vs 8.65e-11), which validates this implementation against it.  Only
  its 'linear (correct)' scheme did not reproduce, and the fifth scheme above identifies why: weighting by
  1/(sigma g_obs)^2 -- the variance of g_obs rather than of the REGRESSAND g_bar^req -- returns
  9.5997e-11, the proposal's 9.397e-11 to 0.009 dex.  That weight is not
  the inverse variance of the quantity being regressed; the derived weight is, and it returns 3.9853e-11,
  -0.371 dex from canonical.  The proposal's 'canonical footing to +0.002 dex' is
  therefore a property of that weight choice and must not be quoted as a measurement.
  [PASS] 1C ⚠ AGAINST THE PROPOSAL, AND THE SHARPEST RESULT IN THIS SCRIPT: the headline agreement with the canonical footing is produced by weighting with the variance of g_obs instead of the variance of the regressand.  The statistically derived weight moves the answer by more than a third of a dex.  This check fails if the two weights agree, i.e. if the choice did not matter after all   (1/(sigma g_obs)^2 gives 9.600e-11 (+0.011 dex from canonical); the derived Phi^2/(sigma g_bar^req)^2 gives 3.985e-11 (-0.371 dex); they differ by +0.382 dex)
  [PASS] 1B and the same regression pins Upsilon far better than it pins a_0 -- the slope is well determined where the intercept condition is not   (Upsilon moves 0.095 dex across the same four schemes where a_0 moves 0.569 dex; Upsilon = 0.498-0.620 against SPS 0.5 +- 0.1)

