  with 0.13 dex log-normal scatter on g_obs: unweighted                                   bias +0.0689 dex
  with 0.13 dex log-normal scatter on g_obs: linear, equal weight per galaxy              bias -0.0126 dex
  [PASS] 2A ⚠ THE SIMULATION SETTLES IT AND THE ANSWER IS NOT FLATTERING: on noiseless synthetics every scheme recovers the injected a_0, so the 0.15 dex spread on real data is NOT a weighting BIAS -- it is the data disagreeing with the model differently in different acceleration ranges, which is a worse problem than a weighting bug.  This check fails if some scheme is biased on noiseless data, which would have been the benign explanation   (max |bias| over all schemes and all three injected a_0 = 0.0000 dex)
  [PASS] 2B and log-normal scatter on g_obs does move the linear scheme, in the direction and roughly the size that explains part of the real spread   (linear scheme bias under 0.13 dex scatter = -0.0396 dex; log scheme -0.0418 dex)

==============================================================================================================================
PART 3 -- THE LEVERS.  Upsilon must be EXACTLY zero by construction; distance must be large, negative,
           and -- against the proposal -- NOT exactly -2, because SPARC is not deep-MOND.
==============================================================================================================================
  d log a_0 / d log Upsilon = +0.000000    (a_0 3.9853e-11 -> 3.9853e-11)
  d log a_0 / d log D       = -1.5089    (a_0 3.9853e-11 -> 2.4784e-11)
  d log a_0 / d log sin i   = -3.0323    (a_0 3.9853e-11 -> 2.2928e-11)
  [PASS] 3A THE UPSILON LEVER IS EXACTLY ZERO, and it is zero for a structural reason: Upsilon is the regression SLOPE and cannot appear in the condition on the INTERCEPT.  No stellar M/L is an input to this pipeline at all   (d log a_0/d log Upsilon = +0.00e+00)
  [PASS] 3B ⚠ AND THE PRICE IS THE MIRROR IMAGE: g_bar^req is built from g_obs, which carries 1/D, while both regressors are D-invariant, so the distance lever is large and negative.  ⚠ THE PROPOSAL'S '-2 EXACTLY' IS THE DEEP-LIMIT VALUE, NOT THE MEASURED ONE: -2 holds only where g_bar^req = g_obs^2/a_0, and SPARC's points straddle the transition, where the exponent runs between -2 (deep) and 0 (Newtonian, where a_0 has dropped out and cannot absorb a distance error at all).  The measured lever must therefore land strictly between -2 and 0, and this check fails if it does not   (d log a_0/d log D = -1.509, between the deep-limit -2 and the Newtonian 0; the proposal's 'exactly -2' is the idealisation)

==============================================================================================================================
PART 4 -- THE RESTATEMENT TEST, EXECUTED: the deep limit run beside the full kernel
==============================================================================================================================
  DERIVATION.  v^4 = G M_b a_0  <=>  g_obs^2 = a_0 g_bar  =>  g_bar^req = g_obs^2/a_0, so the regression
  becomes  g_obs^2/a_0 - g_gas = Upsilon g_star,1 -- v^4 = G M_b a_0 with M_b split into gas and stars and
  Upsilon moved into one coefficient.  THE DERIVATION CLOSES.  is_restatement = True.
  Credit: Stark, McGaugh & Schombert 2009 (AJ 138, 392) for the gas-dominated BTFR zero-point method.
  ⚠ AND THE MEASUREMENT OF THAT CONTENT IS SHARPER THAN EXPECTED.  In the deep limit the weights scale as
  a_0^2 and cancel, so the intercept is EXACTLY linear in 1/a_0:  I(a_0) = A/a_0 - B  with
     A = intercept of g_obs^2 on g_star,1 = -1.8092e-21      B = intercept of g_gas on g_star,1 = +2.2319e-12
  A and B have OPPOSITE SIGNS, so A/B = -8.106e-10 is NEGATIVE and the deep-limit estimator has NO
  positive root: on SPARC the pure v^4 = G M_b a_0 form of this regression cannot be solved AT ALL, for any
  a_0 (scanned 2.0e-12 to 5.0e-09; the intercept is negative throughout).  Full kernel: 3.9853e-11.
  [PASS] 4A ⚠ THE DERIVATION CLOSES -- IS_RESTATEMENT = True -- BUT THE RESTATEMENT ITSELF HAS NO SOLUTION HERE, which is a sharper statement than the proposal's '+0.10 dex correction'.  The transition term is not a small correction to a working estimator; it is what makes the estimator solvable, because SPARC's points are not deep-MOND.  This check fails if the deep limit turns out to have a root after all   (deep-limit intercept has no zero crossing (A = -1.809e-21, B = +2.232e-12, A/B = -8.11e-10 < 0); full kernel solves at 3.985e-11)

